Reject an out-of-range graph index on the RESCUE path too.

Companion to 0014, which guarded the three AI routes to the same defect. This is
the fourth route, found by auditing the callers of DriveGraph::getAngleToNext()
after 0014 was verified.

LinearWorld::getRescuePositionIndex() is declared `unsigned int` but returns
getTrackSector(kart_id)->getCurrentGraphNode(), which is Graph::UNKNOWN_SECTOR
(-1) exactly when the kart is off the drive graph -- and being off the graph is
the usual reason a rescue is running in the first place. So the condition that
produces the invalid sector and the code that consumes it are the same event,
which makes this the most suspicious of the four routes even though 0014's three
guards were enough to take the observed crash rate from 1 in 3 to 0 in 12.

Everything downstream indexes an array with the value:

  * Graph::get()->getQuad(index)->isIgnored(), immediately below, before any
    check; and
  * via the caller (World::rescueKartAfterRescue / moveKartAfterRescue),
    getRescueTransform(index) -> getNode(index) twice, plus
    Track::getAngle(index) -> DriveGraph::getAngleToNext(n, 0).

DriveGraph::getNode(unsigned) bounds-checks only with
assert(j < m_all_nodes.size()), compiled out under NDEBUG, so the index reads
far past m_all_nodes and dynamic_cast dereferences the garbage as a Node* --
reaching the exact fault site observed on hardware, getAngleToNext.

Fixed by falling back to findOutOfRoadSector(), which is what the ignored-quad
branch immediately below already does for its own bad-index case, so this keeps
the function's existing idiom rather than inventing a new failure mode.

Six clean trials on 0014 say its three guards suffice in practice; they do not
make this conversion sound. Compile-verified (single-object build).

--- a/src/modes/linear_world.cpp
+++ b/src/modes/linear_world.cpp
@@ -828,6 +828,22 @@
     // raycast to detect where it is driving on (--> potential rescue loop)
     int index = getTrackSector(kart_id)->getCurrentGraphNode();
 
+    /* getCurrentGraphNode() is Graph::UNKNOWN_SECTOR (-1) exactly when the kart
+     * is off the drive graph -- which is the usual reason a rescue is happening
+     * at all. Everything downstream indexes an array with this value:
+     * getQuad(index) immediately below, and, via the caller,
+     * getRescueTransform() -> getNode(index) and Track::getAngle(index) ->
+     * DriveGraph::getAngleToNext(). DriveGraph::getNode() bounds-checks only
+     * with an assert() compiled out under NDEBUG, so -1 (0xFFFFFFFF once this
+     * function's unsigned return type converts it) reads far out of bounds and
+     * the garbage is dereferenced as a Node*. Fall back the same way the
+     * ignored-quad case just below already does. */
+    if (index == Graph::UNKNOWN_SECTOR || index < 0 ||
+        (unsigned int)index >= Graph::get()->getNumNodes())
+    {
+        return Graph::get()->findOutOfRoadSector(kart->getFrontXYZ());
+    }
+
     // Do not rescue to an ignored quad, find another (non-ignored) quad
     if (Graph::get()->getQuad(index)->isIgnored())
     {
